# DSSO + MFA + OIDC Token Flow
## Sequence of Steps — Dos and Don'ts

---

## Step 1 — User Hits Your App
- App checks if a valid Access Token exists in JS memory
- If not → redirect to IdP
- ✅ Do not store anything yet at this point

---

## Step 2 — DSSO Kicks In Silently
- IdP detects Windows desktop identity automatically in the background
- No username, no password prompt — user feels nothing
- ✅ OS logged-in session handles identity transparently

---

## Step 3 — MFA Challenge Appears
- Identity confirmed via DSSO, but IdP still needs second factor
- User sees only the MFA prompt — push notification or OTP
- User approves it
- ✅ This is the only interaction the user ever has during first login

---

## Step 4 — IdP Issues Tokens
- IdP sends back two tokens to your app
- **Access Token** — valid for 30 minutes
- **Refresh Token** — must be configured for at least 1 day validity
- ✅ Receive both tokens on your **backend**, not directly in browser
- ❌ Never let Refresh Token touch the browser JS layer at all
- ❌ Never expose Refresh Token in API response body to frontend

---

## Step 5 — Storing the Tokens
- ✅ Access Token → keep in **JS memory** (simple variable) on frontend
- ✅ Refresh Token → store in **HttpOnly Secure cookie** set by your backend
- ❌ Never store Refresh Token in **localStorage or sessionStorage** — XSS attack can steal it
- ❌ Never store Refresh Token in a **regular JS-readable cookie**
- ❌ Never store Access Token in **localStorage** — memory is safer

---

## Step 6 — Making API Calls
- ✅ Decode Access Token JWT and check expiry before every call
- ✅ If expiry is within ~10 seconds → refresh proactively before calling
- ✅ Send Access Token as **Authorization Bearer header** in every request
- ❌ Never send Access Token as a URL query parameter — gets logged in server logs

---

## Step 7 — Access Token Expires (after 30 min)
- App gets a 401 from the API
- ✅ Catch that 401 → silently call your backend to refresh
- ✅ Backend reads Refresh Token from HttpOnly cookie → calls IdP → gets new Access Token
- ✅ Return new Access Token to frontend JS memory only
- ❌ Do not show login screen to user — must be completely invisible to them
- ❌ Do not send Refresh Token to frontend even during this exchange

---

## Step 8 — User Closes Browser / Comes Back Within 1 Day
- JS memory is wiped — Access Token is gone, that is fine
- ✅ HttpOnly cookie with Refresh Token is still there — browser persists it
- ✅ User hits the app → backend reads cookie → calls IdP with Refresh Token → gets new Access Token
- ✅ User goes straight in — no DSSO, no MFA again
- ❌ Do not ask user to log in again just because they closed the tab

---

## Step 9 — Refresh Token Expires (after 1 day)
- Backend tries to use Refresh Token → IdP rejects it
- ✅ Clear the HttpOnly cookie
- ✅ Redirect user back to IdP → DSSO kicks in silently again → MFA prompt appears
- ✅ On success → fresh Access Token + fresh Refresh Token → store again as before
- ❌ Do not retry with expired Refresh Token in a loop — handle the error cleanly and redirect

---

## Step 10 — User Explicitly Logs Out
- ✅ Backend clears the HttpOnly cookie
- ✅ Call IdP logout endpoint to invalidate Refresh Token server side
- ✅ Clear Access Token from JS memory
- ❌ Just clearing frontend memory is not enough — Refresh Token must be revoked at IdP too

---

## Quick Summary Card

| What | Where to Store | What Not to Do |
|---|---|---|
| Access Token | JS memory only | localStorage, sessionStorage, cookie |
| Refresh Token | HttpOnly Secure cookie (backend set) | Anywhere JS can read it |
| User identity / claims | JS memory (decoded from Access Token) | localStorage |

---

## Lifecycle in One Line

```
DSSO (silent) → MFA (user sees this once) → Access Token (30 min, memory)
→ Refresh Token (1 day, HttpOnly cookie) → Silent refresh on expiry
→ After 1 day → DSSO + MFA again
```
